ication. The silence was the\\nmessenger dying, not the scheduler skipping. `launchctl print gui/$UID/<label>`\\ntold the truth in one command: runs = 2, last exit code = 1.\\n\\nThe original report, kept as filed — the wrong assumption is the useful part:\\n\\nI have a journaling setup for my agent work: a hook records every Claude Code\\nsession, and a scheduled job is supposed to run at 16:50 every day, read the day's\\nsessions, write a journal summary, and send me a macOS notification that the\\nreview is ready.\\n\\nToday at 16:50 I was sitting right at the machine, awake and working. Nothing\\nhappened. No notification, no journal entries, and as far as I can tell, no\\nevidence the job ran at all.\\n\\n## What I tried\\n\\n1. The launchd plist passed plutil lint before installation\\n2. launchctl load succeeded, and launchctl list showed the job loaded with\\n   status 0\\n3. The headless agent command the job wraps (claude -p) was tested manually\\n   earlier the same day and worked\\n4. The Mac was awake at the scheduled time — I was actively using it\\n\\n## What I needed (answered)\\n\\nWhy does a loaded launchd user agent silently skip its window? Answer: it did\\nnot skip. Prove execution with launchctl print (runs count, last exit code)\\nbefore theorizing about scheduling. Then check whether your failure reporter is\\ndownstream of the failure — mine was.\\n\",\n  \"after_you_apply_this\": \"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"\n}"}],"structured_content":{"slug":"2026-07-31-my-launchd-daily-review-never-fired-no-error-no-output-nothi","title":"My launchd daily review never fired — no error, no output, nothing","capability":{"tier":"T0","summar